#: ../../../Security.md:56
msgid "Criteria"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:58
msgid ""
"Nominees for LLVM Security Response Group membership should fall in one of "
"these groups:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:60
msgid "Individual contributors:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:62
msgid ""
"Specializes in fixing compiler-based security related issues or often "
"participates in their exploration and resolution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:63 ../../../Security.md:69
msgid ""
"Has a track record of finding security vulnerabilities and responsible "
"disclosure of those vulnerabilities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:64 ../../../Security.md:70
msgid ""
"Is a compiler expert who has specific interests in knowing about, resolving, "
"and preventing future security vulnerabilities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:65
msgid ""
"Has actively contributed non-trivial code to the LLVM project in the last "
"year."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:67
msgid "Researchers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:72
msgid "Vendor contacts:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:74
msgid ""
"Represents an organization or company which ships products that include "
"their own copy of LLVM. Due to their position in the organization, the "
"nominee has a reasonable need to know about security issues and disclosure "
"embargoes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:76
msgid ""
"Additionally, the following are necessary but not sufficient criteria for "
"membership in the LLVM Security Response Group:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:78
msgid ""
"If already in the LLVM Security Response Group, has actively participated in "
"one (if any) security issue in the last year."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:79
msgid ""
"If already in the LLVM Security Response Group, has actively participated in "
"most membership discussions in the last year."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:80
msgid ""
"If already in the LLVM Security Response Group, has actively participated in "
"writing or reviewing a transparency report in the last year."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:81
msgid ""
"When employed by a company or other entity, the parent entity has no more "
"than three members already in the LLVM Security Response Group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:82
msgid ""
"When nominated as a vendor contact, their position with that vendor remains "
"the same as when originally nominated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:83
msgid ""
"Nominees are trusted by existing LLVM Security Response Group members to "
"keep communications embargoed while still active."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:85
msgid "Nomination process"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:87
msgid ""
"Anyone who feels they meet these criteria can nominate themselves, or may be "
"nominated by a third party such as an existing LLVM Security Response Group "
"member. The nomination should state whether the nominee is nominated as an "
"individual, researcher, or as a vendor contact. It should clearly describe "
"the grounds for nomination."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:89
msgid ""
"For the moment, nominations are generally proposed, discussed, and voted on "
"using a GitHub pull request. An [example nomination is available here]"
"[example nomination is available here]. The use of pull requests helps keep "
"membership discussions open, transparent, and easily accessible to LLVM "
"developers in many ways. If, for any reason, a fully-world-readable "
"nomination seems inappropriate, you may reach out to the LLVM Security "
"Response Group via the [report a vulnerability][report a vulnerability] "
"route, and a discussion can be had about the best way to approach "
"nomination, given the constraints that individuals are under."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:91
msgid "Choosing new members"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:93
msgid ""
"If a nomination for LLVM Security Response Group membership is supported by "
"a majority of existing LLVM Security Response Group members, then it carries "
"within five business days unless an existing member of the Security Response "
"Group objects. If an objection is raised, the LLVM Security Response Group "
"members should discuss the matter and try to come to consensus; failing "
"this, the nomination will succeed only by a two-thirds supermajority vote of "
"the LLVM Security Response Group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:95
msgid "Accepting membership"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:97
msgid ""
"Before new LLVM Security Response Group membership is finalized, the "
"successful nominee should accept membership and agree to abide by this "
"security policy, particularly [Privileges and Responsibilities of LLVM "
"Security Response Group Members](#privileges-and-responsibilities-of-llvm-"
"security-response-group-members) below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:99
msgid "Keeping Membership Current"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:101
msgid ""
"At least every six months, the LLVM Security Response Group applies the "
"above criteria. The membership list is pruned accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:102
msgid ""
"Any LLVM Security Response Group member can ask that the criteria be applied "
"within the next five business days."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:103
msgid ""
"If a member of the LLVM Security Response Group does not act in accordance "
"with the letter and spirit of this policy, then their LLVM Security Response "
"Group membership can be revoked by a majority vote of the members, not "
"including the person under consideration for revocation. After a member "
"calls for a revocation vote, voting will be open for five business days."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:104
msgid ""
"Emergency suspension: an LLVM Security Response Group member who blatantly "
"disregards the LLVM Security Policy may have their membership temporarily "
"suspended on the request of any two members. In such a case, the requesting "
"members should notify the LLVM Security Response Group with a description of "
"the offense. At this point, membership will be temporarily suspended for "
"five business days, pending outcome of the vote for permanent revocation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:105
msgid ""
"The LLVM Board may remove any member from the LLVM Security Response Group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:107
msgid "Transparency Report"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:109
msgid ""
"Every year, the LLVM Security Response Group must publish a transparency "
"report. The intent of this report is to keep the community informed by "
"summarizing the disclosures that have been made public in the last year. It "
"shall contain a list of all public disclosures, as well as statistics on "
"time to fix issues, length of embargo periods, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:111
msgid ""
"The transparency reports are published at {doc}`SecurityTransparencyReports`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:113
msgid "Privileges and Responsibilities of LLVM Security Response Group Members"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:115
msgid "Access"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:117
msgid ""
"LLVM Security Response Group members will be subscribed to a private "
"[Discussion Medium](#discussion-medium). It will be used for technical "
"discussions of security issues, as well as process discussions about matters "
"such as disclosure timelines and group membership. Members have access to "
"all security issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:119
msgid "Confidentiality"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:121
msgid ""
"Members of the LLVM Security Response Group will be expected to treat LLVM "
"security issue information shared with the group as confidential until "
"publicly disclosed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:123
msgid ""
"Members should not disclose security issue information to non-members unless "
"both members are employed by the same vendor of a LLVM based product, in "
"which case information can be shared within that organization on a need-to-"
"know basis and handled as confidential information normally is within that "
"organization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:124
msgid ""
"If the LLVM Security Response Group agrees, designated members may share "
"issues with vendors of non-LLVM based products if their product suffers from "
"the same issue. The non-LLVM vendor should be asked to respect the issue’s "
"embargo date, and to not share the information beyond the need-to-know "
"people within their organization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:125
msgid ""
"If the LLVM Security Response Group agrees, key experts can be brought in to "
"help address particular issues. The key expert should be asked to respect "
"the issue’s embargo date, and to not share the information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:127
msgid "Disclosure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:129
msgid ""
"Following the process below, the LLVM Security Response Group decides on "
"embargo date for public disclosure for each Security issue. An embargo may "
"be lifted before the agreed-upon date if all vendors planning to ship a fix "
"have already done so, and if the reporter does not object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:131
msgid "Collaboration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:133
msgid "Members of the LLVM Security Response Group are expected to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:135
msgid "Promptly share any LLVM vulnerabilities they become aware of."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:136
msgid "Volunteer to drive issues forward."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:137
msgid "Help evaluate the severity of incoming issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:138
msgid "Help write and review patches to address security issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:139
msgid "Participate in the member nomination and removal processes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:143
msgid "Discussion Medium"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:145
msgid ""
"The medium used to host LLVM Security Response Group discussions is security-"
"sensitive. It should therefore run on infrastructure which can meet our "
"security expectations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:147
msgid ""
"We use [GitHub's mechanism to privately report security vulnerabilities]"
"[github's mechanism to privately report security vulnerabilities] to have "
"security discussions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:149
msgid "File security issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:150
msgid "Discuss security improvements to LLVM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:152
msgid ""
"We also occasionally need to discuss logistics of the LLVM Security Response "
"Group itself:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:154
msgid "Nominate new members."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:155
msgid "Propose member removal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:156
msgid "Suggest policy changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:158
msgid ""
"We often have these discussions publicly, in our {ref}`monthly public sync-"
"up call <online-sync-ups>` and on the Discourse forums. For internal or "
"confidential discussions, we also use a private mailing list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:160
msgid "Process"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:162
msgid ""
"The following process occurs on the discussion medium for each reported "
"issue:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:164
msgid ""
"A security issue reporter (not necessarily an LLVM contributor) reports an "
"issue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:165
msgid ""
"Within two business days, a member of the LLVM Security Response Group is "
"put in charge of driving the issue to an acceptable resolution. This "
"champion doesn’t need to be the same person for each issue. This person can "
"self-nominate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:166
msgid ""
"Members of the LLVM Security Response Group discuss in which circumstances "
"(if any) an issue is relevant to security, and determine if it is a security "
"issue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:167
msgid ""
"Negotiate an embargo date for public disclosure, with a default minimum time "
"limit of ninety days."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:168
msgid ""
"LLVM Security Response Group members can recommend that key experts be "
"pulled in to specific issue discussions. The key expert can be pulled in "
"unless there are objections from other LLVM Security Response Group members."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:169
msgid "Patches are written and reviewed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:170
msgid ""
"Backporting security patches from recent versions to old versions cannot "
"always work. It is up to the LLVM Security Response Group to decide if such "
"backporting should be done, and how far back."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:171
msgid ""
"The LLVM Security Response Group figures out how the LLVM project’s own "
"releases, as well as individual vendors’ releases, can be timed to patch the "
"issue simultaneously."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:172
msgid ""
"Embargo date can be delayed or pulled forward at the LLVM Security Response "
"Group’s discretion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:173
msgid "The issue champion obtains a CVE entry from [MITRE][mitre]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:174
msgid ""
"Once the embargo expires, the patch is posted publicly according to LLVM’s "
"usual code review process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:175
msgid ""
"All security issues (as well as nomination / removal discussions) become "
"public within approximately fourteen weeks of the fix landing in the LLVM "
"repository. Precautions should be taken to avoid disclosing particularly "
"sensitive data included in the report (e.g. username and password pairs)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:177
msgid "Changes to the Policy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:179
msgid ""
"The LLVM Security Policy may be changed by majority vote of the LLVM "
"Security Response Group. Such changes also need to be approved by the LLVM "
"Board."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:181
msgid "What is considered a security issue?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:183
msgid ""
"We define \"security-sensitive\" to mean that a discovered bug or "
"vulnerability may require coordinated disclosure, and therefore should be "
"reported to the LLVM Security Response group rather than publishing in the "
"public bug tracker."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:187
msgid ""
"The LLVM Project has a significant amount of code, and not all of it is "
"considered security-sensitive. This is particularly true because LLVM is "
"used in a wide variety of circumstances: there are different threat models, "
"untrusted inputs differ, and the environment LLVM runs in is varied. "
"Therefore, what the LLVM Project considers a security issue is what its "
"members have signed up to maintain securely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:194
msgid ""
"As this security process matures, members of the LLVM community can propose "
"that a part of the codebase be designated as security-sensitive (or no "
"longer security-sensitive). This requires a rationale, and buy-in from the "
"LLVM community as for any RFC. In some cases, parts of the codebase could be "
"handled as security-sensitive but need significant work to get to the stage "
"where that's manageable. The LLVM community will need to decide whether it "
"wants to invest in making these parts of the code securable, and maintain "
"these security properties over time. In all cases the LLVM Security Response "
"Group {ref}`should be consulted <security-group-discussion-medium>`, since "
"they'll be responding to security issues filed against these parts of the "
"codebase."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:205
msgid ""
"The security-sensitive parts of the LLVM Project currently are the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:207
msgid ""
"Code generation: most miscompilations are not security sensitive. However, a "
"miscompilation where there are clear indications that it can result in the "
"produced binary becoming significantly easier to exploit could be considered "
"security sensitive, and should be reported to the security response group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:211
msgid ""
"Run-time libraries: only parts of the run-time libraries are considered "
"security-sensitive. The parts that are not considered security-sensitive are "
"documented below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:215
msgid ""
"The following parts of the LLVM Project are currently treated as non-"
"security sensitive:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:218
msgid ""
"LLVM's language frontends, analyzers, optimizers, and code generators for "
"which a malicious input can cause undesirable behavior. For example, a "
"maliciously crafted C, Rust or bitcode input file can cause arbitrary code "
"to execute in LLVM. These parts of LLVM haven't been hardened, and handling "
"untrusted code usually also includes running utilities such as make which "
"can more readily perform malicious things. For example, vulnerabilities in "
"clang, clangd, or the LLVM optimizer in a JIT caused by untrusted inputs are "
"not security-sensitive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:227
msgid ""
"The following parts of the run-time libraries are explicitly not considered "
"security-sensitive:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:230
msgid ""
"parts of the run-time libraries that are not meant to be included in "
"production binaries. For example, most sanitizers are not considered "
"security-sensitive as they are meant to be used during development only, not "
"in production."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:234
msgid ""
"for libc and libc++: if a user calls library functionality in an undefined "
"or otherwise incorrect way, this will most likely not be considered a "
"security issue, unless the libc/libc++ documentation explicitly promises to "
"harden or catch that specific undefined behaviour or incorrect usage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:238
msgid ""
"unwinding and exception handling: the implementations are not hardened "
"against malformed or malicious unwind or exception handling data. This is "
"not considered security sensitive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../Security.md:242
msgid ""
"Note that both the explicit security-sensitive and explicit non-security "
"sensitive lists can change over time. If you're not sure whether an issue is "
"in-scope for this security process or not, err towards assuming that it is. "
"The Security Response Group might agree or disagree and will explain its "
"rationale in the report, as well as update this document through the above "
"process."
msgstr ""
